"<strong>Using the virtual apparatus.</strong> Work in the AC analysis mode with predictions and answers hidden (tick Blind: hide predictions and answers under Lab), starting from the Below resonance setup. To set a frequency, select the AC source and type it in the Frequency f box under Selected; the Frequency f slider under Source and frequency is logarithmic and coarse, so type each frequency near the peak and the half power points rather than dragging to it. Press Record reading (under Frequency response readings) at each frequency; it stores \\(f\\), the source \\(V_0\\) and rms voltage, the rms current, the phase, \\(\\lvert Z\\rvert\\), the average power, the part values and the rms voltage across each part, all as the panel shows them. The table on the page shows \\(f\\), \\(I_{\\rm rms}\\) in mA, phase and power; the CSV has every column, in SI units. Check each new row against the readout and delete any row taken by mistake. Rows from different circuits can share the table, since every row keeps its part values; a sweep recorded after clearing goes into its own copy of the Data table. Press Export to worksheet to send the table here; the CSV that goes with it starts with comment lines that list the circuit and mark the data as simulated. Use Go to \\(f_0\\) only after you have found the peak yourself: Blind hides it and the Q readout, so untick Blind to use it, and tick it again before the next sweep. To compare the steady state traces, put the voltage and current on the scope (select a part, then Scope V or Scope I) and press Export scope CSV.",
